	doi = {10.1109/EESPE68405.2026.11649204},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048658954?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Aiming at the common problems of incomplete reliability requirements, limited scene coverage and excessive dependence on empirical experience in the development of military equipment software, this paper proposes a software reliability requirement completeness confirmation method based on mission profile and failure mode. On the basis of constructing a structured mission profile library and standardized FMEA failure mode library, a two-dimensional matrix traversal mechanism is adopted to derive refined scenario-level reliability requirements. Combined with requirement merging abstraction, priority classification and engineering feasibility screening, high-quality standardized requirement sets are formed. Finally, expert systematic review and bidirectional traceability matrix verification are utilized to realize full-process closed-loop control of requirement completeness. The engineering case of ship-borne cooperative air defense mission verifies that the method can effectively cover extreme working conditions and various system failure modes, eliminate requirement omission defects, and embed active reliability design ideas into the whole software development lifecycle. It provides a feasible and standardized technical approach for improving the completeness, rationality and engineering practicability of reliability requirements for complex military equipment software. © 2026 IEEE.},
