	abstract = {Collaboration in engineering design is essential for addressing complex engineering challenges. Requirements elicitation serves as a crucial stage in ensuring that designs align with stakeholder needs. This study examines the relationship between collaboration dynamics and requirements elicitation by exploring how team size affects preservice engineers' ability to generate complete requirements. Completeness is evaluated based on how well the requirements capture necessary functionalities, behaviors, constraints, and specifications vital to the final product or system. To investigate this, an experiment was conducted with first-year students enrolled in an engineering and computer science course at a large public university. A total of 1,208 participants were grouped into 378 teams of varying sizes—one, three, and six members—to assess the impact of team size on the completeness of requirements generated. Each team was tasked with eliciting and documenting requirements for a given engineering design problem. The requirements were analyzed by examining the parts of speech within each requirement. The results indicate that Teams of One rely more on objects and modifiers, likely due to the absence of peer refinement. While Teams of 3 and Teams of 6 prioritize modal terms and target values, suggesting that collaboration helps teams refine vague language into more structured, quantifiable requirements. © 2025 by ASME.},
